Changes 10112026

Cleanup infrastructure
• Added a private cleanup() method that releases every resource acquired by 
  initialize(), is safe to call repeatedly, and null-checks each handle before
  releasing it.
• Replaced the inline teardown block in ~SipLogic with a single call to 
  cleanup(), keeping the sip::Call deletion and vector clear in the destructor.
• Converted all 18 error exits in initialize() to call cleanup(); return false; 
  so no failure path leaks resources.
• Ordered the releases in cleanup() so sinks (valves, selectors) are torn down 
  before their upstream sources.
• Moved the startupfinished = false; reset into cleanup() so it also applies 
  when initialize() aborts.

Previously missing releases
• Added delete mlogicconout;, which was allocated in initialize() but never 
  released.
• Added delete mouttosip;, which was allocated in initialize() but never 
  released.
• Added delete minfromsip;, which was allocated in initialize() but never 
  released.
• Added delete squelchdet;, which was missing from the destructor entirely.
• Added delete sipselector;, which was likewise missing from the destructor.

Regex handles
• Added regfree() plus delete and a null assignment before the exit in all
  four regex error paths, so a regext whose regcomp() failed is never passed to
  regfree().
• Made cleanup() skip regex handles that are null, so a released handle is not
  touched a second time.

Call lifecycle
• Added delete call; to both exit paths in onIncomingCall (invalid caller, 
  rejected call), where a sip::Call was created but neither registered nor freed.
• Added delete call; to the catch block in makeCall, where a call object was 
  left behind when makeCall() threw.
• Added a found flag to unregisterCall() so the object is deleted only when the 
  pointer was actually present in calls.

pjsua / media handling
• Converted AudioMedia from the deprecated registerMediaPort() to 
  registerMediaPort2() with an application-owned pjcachingpool, so the pool 
  lifetime is under our control.
• Added an idempotent unregisterPort() method to AudioMedia and call it from the
  destructor, from unregisterCall() and as a safety net in cleanup().
• Added stopTransmit() in both directions before tearing down media, so no 
  transfer chain survives the call it belonged to.
• Added ep.libDestroy() at the end of cleanup(), guarded by a new epinitialized 
  flag, so pjsua is shut down explicitly before the Endpoint member is destroyed
  implicitly.
• Derived sip::Endpoint from pj::Endpoint and overrode onAudioMediaOpCompleted() 
  to be notified when asynchronous audio media operations finish.

Smaller correctness fixes
• Changed the msiploglevel range check to > 6 only, since the < 0 comparison 
  could never be true for a uint16t.
• Replaced the variable-length array char msg[msgsize] in the four regex blocks 
  with std::vector<char> for portable C++.
• Removed the assert(errsize == msgsize) checks along with the now-unused 
  errsize declaration and its cast.
• Commented out the setAudioCodec declaration in SipLogic.h, which had no 
  definition and would have caused a linker error if called.

Outcome and open items
• The startup assertion grplockdecref: Assertion 'cnt > 0' failed persisted 
  through all of the above and was traced to pjproject itself; it no longer 
  occurs after updating to the current pjproject version from GitHub.
• Still open: onRegState() starts a new dns.lookup() on every registration state
  change, which can overlap the in-flight lookup and was the trigger for the 
  earlier std::futureerror.

### pjproject
- Fixed the `grp_lock_dec_ref: Assertion 'cnt > 0' failed` abort on shutdown, 
  which originated in pjproject's transaction layer rather than in this logic; 
  resolved by updating pjproject to the current version from GitHub.
- The startup assertion `grp_lock_dec_ref: Assertion 'cnt > 0' failed` traced to
  pjproject itself rather than to this logic: the transport's group lock was 
  decremented one time too many during teardown, surfacing in `tsx_shutdown()` 
  via `mod_tsx_layer_stop()`. Updating to the current pjproject version from 
  GitHub resolves it; no change to this logic was required.

